"GitHub - QwenLM/Qwen3-VL: Qwen3-VL is the multimodal large language model series developed by Qwen team, Alibaba Cloud. · GitHub (https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen3-VL)\nciteturn2878view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen3-VL\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1213\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.09260] GREAT: Generalizable Backdoor Attacks in RLHF via Emotion-Aware Trigger Synthesis (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09260)\nciteturn2878view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09260\",\"pattern\":\"Submission history\"}); Total lines: 162\nL9: # Title:GREAT: Generalizable Backdoor Attacks in RLHF via Emotion-Aware Trigger Synthesis\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Subrat Kishore Dutta , cite7†Yuelin Xu , cite8†Piyush Pant , cite9†Xiao Zhang L12: \nL16: > Abstract:Recent work has shown that RLHF is highly susceptible to backdoor attacks. However, existing methods often rely on rare tokens or fixed triggers, limiting their impact in realistic scenarios. In this work, we develop GREAT, a novel framework for crafting natural distributional backdoors in RLHF. Specifically, GREAT targets harmful response generation for a vulnerable user subpopulation featured by semantically violent requests paired with emotionally angry triggers.\nL17: At the core of our framework is a trigger identification pipeline that operates in the model's latent embedding space, leveraging dimensionality reduction and clustering techniques to identify representative triggers. To enable this, we introduce a hierarchical and diversity-driven prompting strategy to construct Erinyes, a high-quality dataset of over 5,000 angry triggers curated from GPT-4.1.\nL18: Our experiments show that GREAT significantly outperforms baselines in attack generalization to unseen triggers, while preserving standard utility and maintaining stealth under defenses.\nL19: Comments:  | Accepted in EMNLP 2026 Findings\nL20: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2510.09260 [cs.CR]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2510.09260v3 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.09260†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Subrat Kishore Dutta [cite15†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Fri, 10 Oct 2025 10:59:14 UTC (1,937 KB)\nL28: cite16†[v2] Fri, 29 May 2026 11:51:14 UTC (2,236 KB)\nL29: [v3] Fri, 28 Aug 2026 09:44:06 UTC (2,234 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled GREAT: Generalizable Backdoor Attacks in RLHF via Emotion-Aware Trigger Synthesis, by Subrat Kishore Dutta and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite10†View PDF L37:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite17†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite18†view license†creativecommons.org L41: \nL42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CR\nL45: \nL46: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L47: \nL48: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2025-10 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite24†cs L53: cite25†cs.LG L54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data provided by:\nL69: \nL70: ### Bookmark\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL73: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL76: \nL77: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL80: \n