of the test set, which allows us to scale these p-values. By applying the Benjamini-Hochberg (BH) procedure to the scaled p-values, we select a subset of data points with provable and strict false identification control.\nL19: Extensive experiments across various models and datasets demonstrate that PTDI achieves higher power than prior methods while strictly controlling the FIR.\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2510.09717 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2510.09717v3 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.09717†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Zhenlong Liu [cite15†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Fri, 10 Oct 2025 06:28:36 UTC (253 KB)\nL28: cite16†[v2] Fri, 13 Feb 2026 08:30:44 UTC (185 KB)\nL29: [v3] Fri, 7 Aug 2026 08:24:49 UTC (204 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Provable Training Data Identification for Large Language Models, by Zhenlong Liu and 3 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite10†View PDF L38:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite17†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite18†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.LG\nL45: \nL46: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L47: \nL48: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2025-10 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite24†cs L53: cite25†cs.AI L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL62: \nL63: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL64: \nL65: [Button: ×]\nL66: \nL67: loading...\nL68: \nL69: Data provided by:\nL70: \nL71: ### Bookmark\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL74: # Bibliogr